    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What was implemented\n\n`namesakeCount` on an owner = the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` **and** `lastName`, compared case-insensitively. It's computed at create time on `POST /api/owners`, persisted, and returned via `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n## Files changed\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` (`integer`, `readOnly`, `minimum: 0`) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` JPA column with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added a `countNamesakes(owner)` helper (reusing the existing `normalizeHouseholdField` for case-insensitive comparison) and set `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)` just before saving, alongside the existing `customerCode`/`householdId` logic. Because the count runs over `findAllOwners()` before the new owner is saved, it reflects only pre-existing owners.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (tests run on `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the SQL schema is authoritative).\n\n5. **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 the seed inserts use positional `VALUES` with no column list, so I appended a trailing `NULL` for the new column to keep the counts aligned (this was the cause of the initial \"row column count mismatch\"). h2/postgres seed inserts use explicit column lists and needed no change.\n\nMapStruct auto-maps the matching `namesakeCount` property between `Owner` and `OwnerDto`, so no mapper edit was needed.\n\nTest result: `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` passes (asserts `namesakeCount == 1` for the second of two same-name owners), full suite green \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
